@page "/utility-spacing"

<CatHead Title="Spacing utilities">
    Two families, and deliberately only two: the gap inside a row or column, and a top or bottom
    margin on one element. Everything else is a token.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout-warn">
    <i class="ri-forbid-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">There is no numeric spacing scale</span>
        And there will not be one. The vertical
        rhythm between page sections is the frame's job — see <a href="/frame">Shell</a> — and the
        sizes themselves are <a href="/tokens"><code>--space-*</code></a>.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilitySpacing.Gaps" Title="Gaps" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-row</code> and <code>.sedna-col</code> carry one gap, and these four override it:
        <code>.sedna-gap-0</code> removes it, <code>.sedna-gap-1</code> is the default 8px restated,
        and <code>-2</code> and <code>-3</code> are 12px and 20px. A gap that needs a fifth value is
        usually a layout that wants <a href="/grid">a grid</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.UtilitySpacing.Margins" Title="Margins" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.sedna-mt-0</code> to <code>-3</code> and <code>.sedna-mb-0</code> to <code>-3</code>,
        on the same 0 / 8 / 12 / 20px steps. Top and bottom only, and the zeros do most of the work:
        they are the escape from a margin a library component sets, without overriding that
        component's class.
    </p>
</CatExample>
